WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.324
A fraud model learns from past payments.

2
00:00:05.324 --> 00:00:10.244
Each one is labelled fraud or not once its chargeback arrives, weeks later.

3
00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:17.191
It also learns from features: numbers about the card, like how many chargebacks it had in the last 30 days.

4
00:00:17.367 --> 00:00:22.290
Every night at two, a job writes one row for the card, as of midnight.

5
00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:23.908
For now the count is 0.

6
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:28.359
On 10 October at two in the afternoon, the card is used for fraud.

7
00:00:28.359 --> 00:00:33.585
Its chargeback arrives on the twelfth, so from the thirteenth on, the count is 1.

8
00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:36.826
The easy join takes the card's newest row: 1.

9
00:00:36.826 --> 00:00:40.125
That 1 is the chargeback this very fraud caused.

10
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:46.798
The model learns that fraud comes with a chargeback already on file, which it can never see when it decides.

11
00:00:46.798 --> 00:00:50.468
This is label leakage: the future reaching the training data.

12
00:00:50.667 --> 00:00:55.900
Across 150,000 labels, every one got a value from after its payment.

13
00:00:55.900 --> 00:01:02.289
On that data, the rule "flag a card with a chargeback" catches 98 percent of fraud.

14
00:01:02.289 --> 00:01:03.828
Live, it catches 62.

15
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.515
A point in time join looks back from each label instead.

16
00:01:07.515 --> 00:01:11.971
It takes the newest row that already existed when the payment happened.

17
00:01:12.167 --> 00:01:15.121
For this payment, that's the 10 October row: 0.

18
00:01:15.121 --> 00:01:18.516
Exactly what the model could have read at that moment.

19
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:24.563
Trained on these rows, the same rule catches 62 percent, which is what production gets.

20
00:01:24.563 --> 00:01:29.551
Feature stores like Feast, and DuckDB's ASOF JOIN, do this lookup for you.

21
00:01:29.733 --> 00:01:34.967
Join each label to its features as they were at that moment, never as they are now.
